    "Guardrails are cross-cutting concerns that apply across many parts of the app and should not be duplicated inline everywhere.",
    "Guardrails act on a contract, not implementation details, so the app and guardrails remain separate entities.",
    "Pluggability and composability let you reorder, add, remove, or replace guardrails without rewriting app logic.",
    "Validators are individual guardrails attached to a guard object, and the guard object binds those validators to the application.",
    "This design avoids breaking existing code or missing applications of rules when the app evolves.",
    "Guardrails must be tested independently, not only together with the app, so the rules themselves can be verified.",
    "If guardrails were hardcoded early, they would have to be reimplemented on each iteration as the app evolves.",
    "Testing guardrails independently avoids rework during evaluation and iteration.",
    "The same guardrails can be applied across different application types such as RAG, agentic, and QA chatbot systems without changing app logic.",
    "Guardrails validate inputs and outputs across components like the input, retriever, and generator.",
    "Guardrails interact with the app like an evaluator and provide consistent enforcement.",
    "Guardrails are like gift wrap, separate from the box contents, so inspecting the box or changing contents does not require changing the wrap."
] 

