Statements:
[
    "Generation produces initial synthetic "goldens" or raw QA candidates from the model using the constructed context.",
    "Filtration removes low-quality or unsafe goldens so only those meeting generation standards move forward.",
    "Filtration is controlled by a FiltrationConfig with optional parameters such as a critique model and a threshold.",
    "The critique model scores candidates.",
    "The threshold determines how strictly candidates are kept.",
    "The default threshold is about 0.5, commonly ranging from 0.4 to 0.6.",
    "An example threshold used is 0.6.",
    "Filtration also filters out bad questions that should not be presented to the LLM.",
    "Evolution rewrites or evolves the filtered goldens.",
    "Raw inputs that were filtered out are not evolved.",
    "Evolution increases complexity and realism so the synthetic items become more like human-curated goldens.",
    "The goal of evolution is to make the items more realistic, interesting, and harder to distinguish from human examples.",
    "Each input can be rewritten multiple times during evolution.",
    "The number of rewrites is determined by num_evolution.",
    "The pipeline aims to scale fast while approaching human quality.",
    "The quality target is roughly 80–90% of human quality.",
    "The tradeoff is that some quality is tolerated.",
    "Styling formats the input and output of the evolved synthetic goldens.",
    "Styled outputs are meant to look realistic and usable.",
    "Proper styling makes the synthetic QA pairs more realistic.",
    "Proper styling makes the synthetic QA pairs applicable for downstream testing or training.",
    "JSON is referenced as a format requirement."
] 

