Statements:
[
    "Retriever failures can be categorized into three types.",
    "Coverage/completeness refers to how much of the relevant context the retriever fetches.",
    "The metric for coverage/completeness is contextual recall.",
    "Chunking hyperparameters, top_k, embedding model/embedding stage, and chunking strategy are tunable parameters for coverage/completeness.",
    "If recall is low, increase chunk size to retrieve more information.",
    "Relevancy refers to how relevant the retrieved results are to the query.",
    "The metric for relevancy is contextual relevancy.",
    "Chunk size, overlap, embedding model/embedding stage, top_k, chunking strategy, and prompt/instructions passed to the LLM are tunable parameters for relevancy.",
    "If relevancy is low, decrease chunk size to make chunks more focused.",
    "Ranking/order refers to the correctness of the ordering of retrieved results.",
    "The metric for ranking/order is precision, used to study ranking.",
    "Retriever scoring method, top_k, embedding model, and adding a reranker as a separate component are tunable parameters for ranking/order.",
    "A reranker is typically a cross-encoder.",
    "A reranker recalculates similarities and can improve ranking.",
    "In production, the retriever itself is often evaluated separately, so precision and reranking may be handled separately.",
    "Metrics should be tuned together across the application but treated as targeting different components.",
    "Only the parameters relevant to the failing metric should be adjusted.",
    "Chunking and embedding stages are the primary levers that affect retriever performance."
] 

